==============================================================================
1. Consecutive P-smooth pairs below 10^15 (recursive enumeration) and below 2*10^6 (sieve)
==============================================================================
 P = [2, 3]: 4 pairs below 10^15, largest (8, 9)
 P = [2, 3, 5]: 10 pairs below 10^15, largest (80, 81)
 P = [2, 3, 5, 7]: 23 pairs below 10^15, largest (4374, 4375)
 P = [2, 3, 5, 7, 11]: 40 pairs below 10^15, largest (9800, 9801)
 P = [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13]: 68 pairs below 10^15, largest (123200, 123201)
 counts 4, 10, 23, 40, 68 = OEIS A002071; largest upper members 9, 81, 4375, 9801, 123201 = A117581.
 (Completeness beyond 10^15 is Stormer's theorem, not this computation.)

==============================================================================
2. Unit cells: 2^k - 1 or 2^k + 1 smooth over M, k <= 3000
==============================================================================
 M = [3]: 4 unit cells (k, N): [(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 3), (3, 9)]   [4 of 4 Stormer pairs]
 M = [3, 5]: 6 unit cells (k, N): [(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 5), (3, 9), (4, 15)]   [6 of 10 Stormer pairs]
 M = [3, 5, 7]: 8 unit cells (k, N): [(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7), (3, 9), (4, 15), (6, 63)]   [8 of 23 Stormer pairs]
 M = [3, 5, 7, 11]: 9 unit cells (k, N): [(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7), (3, 9), (4, 15), (5, 33), (6, 63)]   [9 of 40 Stormer pairs]
 M = [3, 5, 7, 11, 13]: 11 unit cells (k, N): [(1, 1), (1, 3), (2, 3), (2, 5), (3, 7), (3, 9), (4, 15), (5, 33), (6, 63), (6, 65), (12, 4095)]   [11 of 68 Stormer pairs]
 -> 4/4, 6/10, 8/23, 9/40, 11/68 confirmed.  NOTE: 'unit cells = Stormer pairs with a pure power
    of two on one side' is true BY DEFINITION (a unit cell is a consecutive pair {2^k, N}); only
    the counts and the finiteness bound carry information.

==============================================================================
3. Mixed-multiplier words by plain iteration (no fixed-point formula).
   For each arrangement of the letters, search x in [-5000, 5000] with: letter 0 applied to
   an even value, letter q applied to an odd value, and the word returning to x.
==============================================================================
 cell 4 - 3: 2 words, 2 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 1 necklaces
      (0, 3): [2, 1]
 cell 8 - 9: 3 words, 3 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 1 necklaces
      (0, 3, 3): [-5, -7, -10]
 cell 4 - 5: 2 words, 2 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 1 necklaces
      (0, 5): [-1, -2]
 cell 16 - 15: 12 words, 12 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 3 necklaces
      (0, 0, 3, 5): [7, 11, 28, 14]
      (0, 0, 5, 3): [20, 10, 5, 13]
      (0, 3, 0, 5): [14, 7, 18, 9]
 cell 8 - 7: 3 words, 3 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 1 necklaces
      (0, 0, 7): [4, 2, 1]
 cell 64 - 63: 60 words, 60 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 10 necklaces
 cell 32 - 33: 20 words, 20 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 4 necklaces
 cell 64 - 65: 30 words, 30 with exactly one consistent integer fixed point, 5 necklaces
 -> 16 - 15: 3 cycles (7,11,28,14 / 5,13,20,10 / 9,14,7,18 up to rotation); 64 - 63: 10.  Confirmed.
    7 is multiplied by 3 in one cycle and by 5 in another: this is a semigroup, not a map.

